  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are mostly sunny at this time with\nsome low to mid clouds streaming across portions of northern and\neast-central Nevada. Winds have developed across central Nevada\nwith gusts of 30 to 40 mph. These winds will eventually diminish\nlater this evening.\n\nFor the rest of today and tonight, upper level low pressure is\ncurrently spinning across southwestern Oregon at this time with a\nshortwave rounding the base of this trough. Upper level ridging is\ncurrently over the mid-western United States. The upper trough\nwill quickly move northeastward tonight and Monday, bringing a\nmore westerly flow to the forecast area. The isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms over parts of the northern and central \nsections will continue into the early evening hours before \ndissipating. Overnight lows will be in the 40s.\n\nFor Monday, the aforementioned upper trough will scoot off to the\nnortheast. Meanwhile, heights across northern and central Nevada\nwill slowly rise during the day. Winds are not expected to be as\nstrong with some occasional breeziness to 20 mph but that is about\nit. Highs will be in the 70s and 80s. Look for a rather chilly\novernight period with mainly clear skies. Given that we are still\nin the growing season, there is a possibility of reaching\ntemperatures below 36 degrees. Some of the colder locales like\nWildhorse is showing thirty-five to forty-five percent probability\nof seeing low temperatures below 36 degrees for the Monday night \nto Tuesday morning time. A frost advisory may be needed for \nportions of northern, south- central Elko counties, along with \nsouthern Lander/Eureka, and White Pine counties for Tuesday \nmorning. Lows will be in the 30s.\n\nModels are now showing a continuation of the rising heights for\nthe Tuesday through Friday timeframe. This will mean afternoon \nhigh temperatures are expected to warm to above normal levels with\nreadings in the low to upper 80s. Winds are expected to be rather\nlight winds, though some occasional afternoon breeziness to 20 \nmph are possible. At this time, no precipitation is expected. Dry \nconditions will continue into the overnight each day with lows in \nthe 40s.\n\nBy Saturday, model simulations are depicting a rather sharp\nshortwave trough diving to the south and across the Pacific\nNorthwest states. Did not make any changes to the current blended\nforecast at this time for the upcoming weekend.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: No changes were made to the\ncurrent NBM forecast. There is high confidence cooler than normal\ntemperatures continuing through roughly Tuesday. \n\nThere is moderate confidence in a 10% to 30% chance for isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms this afternoon across northern and\neastern portions of the forecast area. Confidence is also moderate\nregarding Tuesday's morning low temperatures and the possibility\nof headline cold products being required.\n\nThere is low confidence in the long range forecast as model \nsolutions show a high degree of divergence for the upper pattern \nafter Friday.",
